K10 PASS step between 1.18 and 1.71 Mpc; u_in=0.522, u_out=1.122, chi2/dof=1.26 (3 params) step in (1.18,1.71), chi2/dof<2 :: a step u=u_in (r<r_t), u_out (r>r_t) with r_t fitted fits the eight bins at chi2/dof = 1.26 (constant u: 4.90); it splits them 0-5 | 6-7, i.e. the discontinuity lies in the gap between 1.18 and 1.71 Mpc
K11 PASS 1.42-2.06: 1.284+-0.246; 2.06-3.0: 1.082+-0.121 both <1.2 sigma from 1, differ <1 sigma :: OUTER splits into two sub-bands, both of which are u=1 within 1 sigma and agree with each other: outside the transition there is no further radial structure -- u saturates at dust

====================================================================================================
K12-K14  MECHANISM (i) -- relaxation toward dust: the sign is wrong, and the amplitude is worse
====================================================================================================
K12 PASS t_relax(0.6)/t_relax(2.0)=0.0107 <0.1 (inner relaxes faster) :: any local relaxation with a rate rising with density gives t_relax = t_dyn/rho: 24.437 Gyr at 0.6 Mpc vs 2287.903 Gyr at 2.0 Mpc, ratio 0.0107. The inner medium relaxes ~94x FASTER, so it reaches dust FIRST: relaxation predicts u_inner > u_outer. The measurement is u_inner = 0.335 < 0.987
K13 PASS required t_in/t_out >= 1000000000000; predicted 0.0107; discrepancy 14.0 decades, inverted required>10, predicted<0.1, discrepancy>2 decades :: to have the inner band unrelaxed (u=0.335) and the outer band fully relaxed (u=0.986) the relaxation time must DECREASE outward by a factor 1000000000000; the density scaling makes it INCREASE outward by 94. The two differ by 14.0 decades in the wrong direction
K14 PASS predicted u_INNER=1.000 vs measured 0.441+-0.056 (9.9 sigma) prediction >0.95, >8 sigma from data :: fix the one free amplitude so the outer band is 'fully relaxed' and the inner band comes out at u=1 as well; the INNER measurement excludes that at 9.9 sigma. Relaxation cannot be rescued by its normalisation either

====================================================================================================
K15-K17  MECHANISM (ii) -- phase space: u as a fraction of the momentum distribution
====================================================================================================

  Osipkov-Merritt beta(r) = r^2/(r^2+r_a^2); beta=1/2 exactly at r=r_a
    r_a =  0.229 Mpc (r_s (the only NFW length) ) -> r(beta=1/2) =  0.229 Mpc, beta at 0.38/0.81/1.71/2.49 = 0.733/0.926/0.982/0.992
    r_a =  0.516 Mpc (r200c/2                   ) -> r(beta=1/2) =  0.516 Mpc, beta at 0.38/0.81/1.71/2.49 = 0.352/0.711/0.917/0.959
    r_a =  0.780 Mpc (fitted to u=1/2           ) -> r(beta=1/2) =  0.780 Mpc, beta at 0.38/0.81/1.71/2.49 = 0.192/0.519/0.828/0.911
    the DATA cross u=1/2 at r = 0.780 Mpc (log-linear between the 0.81 and 1.18 Mpc bins)

K15 PASS r(beta=1/2)=r_s=0.229 Mpc vs data 0.780 Mpc (factor 3.4) factor >2 apart :: u=1/2 <=> beta=1/2, and for OM that is r=r_a. The one NFW-motivated choice r_a=r_s puts the crossing at 0.229 Mpc, a factor 3.4 inside the measured crossing at 0.780 Mpc. r_a has to be fitted, so the mechanism predicts nothing
K16 PASS OM chi2/dof=3.86 (r_a=0.33, A=0.73) vs step 1.26 (3 params) chi2/dof>3 and dchi2>10 vs step :: the smooth phase-space reading, u = A beta(r) with two free parameters, is rejected at chi2/dof = 3.86 against 1.26 for the step (which has one MORE parameter, r_t): the data have a discontinuity (dchi2 = 16.8 for one extra parameter) that no single-scale beta(r) produces
K17 PASS max|Z| u=beta: 3.7 (at 0.38 Mpc); u=1-beta: 8.2 both >3 sigma in the eight bins :: with r_a fixed at the observed u=1/2 crossing, u = beta (radial fraction) is wrong by 3.7 sigma at 0.38 Mpc and u = 1-beta (isotropic fraction) by 8.2 sigma at 2.49 Mpc, in opposite directions: no single beta(r) normalisation fits the eight bins

